  }))), "Description (LLM)"), mdx("p", null, "This is a comprehensive survey paper that systematically reviews and analyzes existing research on detecting lexical semantic change using Large Language Models (LLMs). The paper focuses on a specific NLP task called Lexical Semantic Change Detection (LSC) \u2014 which involves identifying how the meanings of words evolve over time across historical corpora (e.g., comparing word usage in 18th-century texts vs. modern texts).\nThe key contributions and scope of the paper include:\nA formal framework for classifying LSC approaches based on three dimensions:"), mdx("p", null, "Meaning representation (e.g., form-based vs. sense-based approaches)\nTime awareness (e.g., methods handling single vs. multiple time intervals)\nLearning modality (e.g., unsupervised vs. supervised techniques)\nA detailed comparison of 40+ LSC methods from recent literature (2019\u20132024), evaluated across 10+ language corpora (e.g., English, German, Spanish, Norwegian). The analysis includes performance metrics like Spearman\u2019s correlation scores (shown in Table 6)."), mdx("p", null, "Critical discussion of challenges in LSC using LLMs, including:"), mdx("p", null, "Scalability issues (handling large corpora/target words)\nInterpretability problems (tracing which specific meanings change)\nRobustness concerns (bias from orthography, model instability, data imbalance)\nFuture directions for the field, such as improving multilingual support, integrating linguistic theory (e.g., semantic change laws), and expanding to real-world applications like historical text analysis."), mdx("p", null, "Why this paper matters\nThis survey is significant because it\u2019s one of the first to provide a structured, evidence-based overview of how LLMs are being applied to a foundational linguistics problem: how word meanings shift over time. Traditional methods for semantic change detection (e.g., using handcrafted features or older NLP models) are being replaced by LLM-based approaches that leverage modern language understanding capabilities. However, this field is still emerging, and the paper helps researchers and practitioners navigate the trade-offs, limitations, and opportunities.\nFor example, the paper reveals that form-based methods (e.g., measuring changes in word embeddings\u2019 distribution) often outperform sense-based methods (e.g., tracking changes in individual word meanings) across most benchmarks \u2014 but sense-based approaches offer better interpretability for linguistic insights."), mdx("p", null, "Summary in 1 sentence for quick reference:\nThis paper surveys how researchers are using Large Language Models to detect how word meanings change over time across historical texts, while identifying key challenges and future pathways for this growing field."), mdx("h5", {
