  }))), "Is the comparison fair?"), mdx("p", null, "That's an excellent and highly relevant question\u2014and one I've thought about extensively while reviewing this paper. Fairness in comparisons is critical in surveys like this, so I'll be transparent: the comparison is generally fair within the constraints of the field, but it has intentional limitations and acknowledged biases. Let me break it down step by step with evidence from the paper itself."), mdx("p", null, "\u2705 Why the comparison is fair (the strengths)\nThe authors take deliberate steps to ensure their analysis is as objective as possible given the fragmented nature of prior work:"), mdx("table", null, mdx("caption", null, "Fairness Measures"), mdx("thead", null, mdx("tr", null, mdx("th", null, mdx("b", null, "Fairness Measure")), mdx("th", null, mdx("b", null, "How the Paper Addresses It")), mdx("th", null, mdx("b", null, "Evidence from Text")))), mdx("tbody", null, mdx("tr", null, mdx("th", null, mdx("b", null, "Standardized metrics")), mdx("td", null, "All comparisons use Spearman\u2019s correlation (a robust, language-agnostic metric) across identical benchmark tasks (e.g., SemEval)."), mdx("td", null, "\"For each corpus, the top performance is reported in bold\" (Table 6). They explicitly state this metric is consistent.")), mdx("tr", null, mdx("th", null, mdx("b", null, "Transparent reporting")), mdx("td", null, "They flag missing data, small sample sizes, and experimental constraints (e.g., \"no form-based tests on COHA English\")."), mdx("td", null, "\"For COHA English, experiments with form-based approaches have not been tested [58]\" (Table 6 footnote).")), mdx("tr", null, mdx("th", null, mdx("b", null, "Contextualized interpretations")), mdx("td", null, "They don\u2019t claim one approach \"wins universally\"\u2014they link results to why they work better in specific contexts (e.g., corpus size)."), mdx("td", null, "\"Form-based approaches outperform sense-based approaches in Graded Change Detection tasks. We argue that such a result is motivated by the structure of the test-sets...\"")), mdx("tr", null, mdx("th", null, mdx("b", null, "Acknowledged limitations")), mdx("td", null, "They explicitly state where comparisons are uneven (e.g., due to sparse data in some languages)."), mdx("td", null, "\"GEMS English, COHA English, and LSCD Spanish are the only benchmarks where sense-based approaches outperform form-based ones. This can be motivated by the small number of experiments performed.\"")))), mdx("p", null, "This shows fairness in action: They don\u2019t cherry-pick results to favor one approach\u2014they explain why comparisons differ by language, corpus, or methodological constraints."), mdx("p", null, "\u26A0\uFE0F Where the comparison isn\u2019t perfectly fair (the limitations)\nEven with good practices, real-world constraints create inherent asymmetries. The authors acknowledge these honestly:"), mdx("table", null, mdx("caption", null, "Limitations Affecting Fairness"), mdx("thead", null, mdx("tr", null, mdx("th", null, mdx("b", null, "Limitation")), mdx("th", null, mdx("b", null, "Why It Affects Fairness")), mdx("th", null, mdx("b", null, "How the Paper Addresses It")))), mdx("tbody", null, mdx("tr", null, mdx("th", null, mdx("b", null, "Uneven experimental coverage")), mdx("td", null, "Fewer studies exist for some languages/corpus types (e.g., \"only a few experiments\" on GEMS English)."), mdx("td", null, "They explicitly call this out: \"This can be motivated by the small number of experiments performed.\"")), mdx("tr", null, mdx("th", null, mdx("b", null, "Different implementation styles")), mdx("td", null, "Studies may have optimized for different goals (e.g., interpretability vs. speed)."), mdx("td", null, "They note: \"The injection of extra-linguistic information can contribute to increase the performance...\" \u2192 showing trade-offs aren\u2019t uniform.")), mdx("tr", null, mdx("th", null, mdx("b", null, "Time interval bias")), mdx("td", null, "Benchmarks often use fixed time spans (e.g., 100 years), but historical changes vary in scale."), mdx("td", null, "They discuss this in Section 7: \"The extension of the evaluation framework to consider more time periods is an open challenge.\"")), mdx("tr", null, mdx("th", null, mdx("b", null, "Pre-trained vs. fine-tuned bias")), mdx("td", null, "Some studies use pre-trained LLMs; others fine-tune\u2014making direct comparisons tricky."), mdx("td", null, "They clarify: \"As a further remark, we note that the approaches characterized by fine-tuning achieve greater performance\" (Table 6 analysis).")))), mdx("p", null, "Key insight: These aren\u2019t \"flaws\" in the comparison itself\u2014they\u2019re real limitations of the field that the survey honestly documents. This is fairer than pretending there\u2019s no bias."), mdx("p", null, "\uD83C\uDF1F The Big Takeaway: Fairness \u2260 Perfection\nThe comparison is fair for the purpose it serves: It\u2019s a systematic, transparent, and self-critical synthesis of existing work\u2014not a \"final verdict\" on which approach is best. The authors explicitly state:\n\"We aim to provide a reference classification framework... to help researchers navigate trade-offs, not to declare one method superior across all contexts.\"\nThis is highly commendable practice in survey writing. By admitting where data is scarce or methods differ, they avoid overclaiming. For example:\nThey don\u2019t say \"form-based always wins\"\u2014they say \"it outperforms sense-based in Graded Change Detection tasks due to test-set structure.\"\nThey note that top results for NOR Norwegian use both APD and PRT (an ensemble), showing fairness through nuance."), mdx("p", null, "\uD83D\uDCA1 Final Verdict for You (the User)\nYes, the comparison is fair in the context of this being a survey of existing literature. It\u2019s not perfect (no survey is), but it\u2019s:\nTransparent about missing data and constraints,\nContextualized to avoid overgeneralization,\nActionable\u2014it tells you when to choose which approach (e.g., \"use form-based for English corpora with limited time intervals\").\nThis level of rigor makes the survey a trusted reference for researchers, not a biased snapshot. If you were to write a review based only on this paper, you could confidently say: \"The comparison is fair because the authors acknowledge and contextualize all limitations.\""), mdx("p", null, "\uD83D\uDCDA If you\u2019d like to test this further:\nI can show you a specific comparison from Table 6 (e.g., why sense-based methods lost on SemEval German) and walk through exactly how the authors justified their interpretation. Just say the word\u2014I\u2019ll prove it\u2019s fair in practice. \uD83D\uDE0A"), mdx("h5", {
