  }))), "Would you use this?  If so, how/where would you use this?"), mdx("p", null, "That's a fantastic question\u2014it shows you're thinking beyond theory to action. As an AI assistant who\u2019s designed to help researchers and practitioners, I would absolutely use this survey paper, and here\u2019s exactly how I\u2019d deploy it in real work (with concrete examples I\u2019d take from it):"), mdx("p", null, "\u2705 Where I\u2019d Use This Paper (and How)\nHere\u2019s a roadmap I\u2019d follow if I were building an NLP tool for historical language analysis (e.g., tracking semantic shifts in medieval French texts):"), mdx("table", null, mdx("caption", null, "How I\u2019d Use This Paper"), mdx("thead", null, mdx("tr", null, mdx("th", null, mdx("b", null, "Stage")), mdx("th", null, mdx("b", null, "How I\u2019d Use This Paper")), mdx("th", null, mdx("b", null, "Why It Matters")))), mdx("tbody", null, mdx("tr", null, mdx("th", null, mdx("b", null, "1. Problem Definition")), mdx("td", null, "I\u2019d start by checking if my goal fits the survey\u2019s taxonomy (e.g., \"I need to detect which specific meanings changed in Shakespearean English \u2192 this points to sense-based approaches\")."), mdx("td", null, "Prevents misalignment: e.g., if I only needed to measure overall change, I\u2019d skip sense-based work.")), mdx("tr", null, mdx("th", null, mdx("b", null, "2. Method Selection")), mdx("td", null, "I\u2019d cross-reference Table 6 to pick the best fit for my corpus (e.g., for a small Norwegian corpus \u2192 I\u2019d prioritize ensemble approaches like APD + PRT from Section 6.3)."), mdx("td", null, "Avoids wasted effort: The survey shows why certain methods fail in specific contexts (e.g., sense-based struggles with sparse data).")), mdx("tr", null, mdx("th", null, mdx("b", null, "3. Model Choice")), mdx("td", null, "I\u2019d test tiny models (e.g., BERT-tiny) first instead of big ones\u2014based on the paper\u2019s finding that they outperform larger models on historical data (SemEval English results)."), mdx("td", null, "Saves time/resources: No need to train massive LLMs when a tiny model already gives better scores.")), mdx("tr", null, mdx("th", null, mdx("b", null, "4. Scalability Planning")), mdx("td", null, "I\u2019d use Section 6.1 to design for scalability (e.g., \"If I\u2019m processing 100k words, I\u2019ll implement random sampling for APD to reduce memory overhead\")."), mdx("td", null, "Prevents project failure: The paper explicitly shows how form-based methods scale better with large corpora.")), mdx("tr", null, mdx("th", null, mdx("b", null, "5. Interpretability Audit")), mdx("td", null, "I\u2019d build in sense-based clustering with refinement (e.g., merging small clusters >2 instances) to explain why a word changed\u2014directly addressing the \"interpretability gap\" noted in Section 6.2."), mdx("td", null, "Turns metrics into insights: Without this, my tool would be a black box for linguists.")))), mdx("p", null, "\uD83D\uDEE0\uFE0F Real-World Example: Building a Tool for Historical Lexicography\nSuppose I\u2019m creating a tool to help lexicographers track semantic changes in 18th-century German (DURel German corpus). Here\u2019s how I\u2019d apply the survey:\nStart with the survey\u2019s classification framework \u2192 I\u2019d confirm my approach fits \"time-aware, ensemble-based, sense-refined\" (top-performing category for DURel German from Table 6).\nPick a tiny model \u2192 Based on the paper\u2019s finding, I\u2019d use ELMo (not BERT-base) to balance speed and historical accuracy.\nAdd clustering refinement \u2192 I\u2019d implement the \"merge clusters <2 instances\" technique from Section 6.2 to make sense-based interpretations reliable.\nTest scalability \u2192 For a full dictionary (10k+ words), I\u2019d use random sampling (Section 6.1) to avoid memory crashes.\nResult: A tool that gives lexicographers explainable semantic shifts\u2014not just correlation scores. This is exactly the \"bridge\" the survey describes between LLMs and linguistic practice."), mdx("p", null, "\uD83C\uDF1F Why This Matters for You (The User)\nIf you\u2019re reading this, I\u2019d recommend using this paper as your first step in any LSC project. Here\u2019s what I\u2019d say to you now:\n\"Don\u2019t jump into coding. Read this survey first\u2014then you\u2019ll know which trade-offs to make (e.g., \u2018I\u2019ll sacrifice interpretability for speed if I\u2019m scraping 1M historical tweets\u2019). It\u2019s the most efficient way to avoid building something that won\u2019t work for real linguists.\""), mdx("p", null, "\uD83D\uDCAC Final Answer in One Sentence (For Your Next Step)\nI would use this survey to guide every decision in building LSC tools: from picking tiny models instead of big ones, to choosing sense-based techniques only when I need interpretability, to never ignoring linguistic theory\u2014because it\u2019s the compass that turns raw LLM metrics into meaningful language history.\nIf you\u2019d like me to draft a specific implementation plan (e.g., \"How to apply this to detect semantic change in Twitter historical data\"), I\u2019ll do it right now. Just say which project interests you most\u2014I\u2019m here to help. \uD83D\uDE0A"), mdx("h5", {
