  }))), "What problems remain and what are the next steps?"), mdx("p", null, "Here\u2019s a precise, actionable breakdown of the key problems that remain and the next steps the authors explicitly identify in this survey paper\u2014based exclusively on their conclusions in Section 7 (\"Challenges and Concluding Remarks\"). I\u2019ve organized this to help you (or your team) prioritize real-world next steps:"), mdx("p", null, "\uD83D\uDD25 Top 5 Remaining Problems & Their Next Steps (From the Authors\u2019 Perspective)"), mdx("table", null, mdx("caption", null, "Outstanding Problems and Next Steps"), mdx("thead", null, mdx("tr", null, mdx("th", null, mdx("b", null, "Problem")), mdx("th", null, mdx("b", null, "What the Paper Says It\u2019s Not Yet Solved")), mdx("th", null, mdx("b", null, "Concrete Next Step")), mdx("th", null, mdx("b", null, "Why It Matters for You")))), mdx("tbody", null, mdx("tr", null, mdx("th", null, mdx("b", null, "1. Meaning evolution tracking")), mdx("td", null, "\"It\u2019s hard to trace which specific meaning changed over multiple time intervals (e.g., 3+ periods).\""), mdx("td", null, "Develop incremental clustering techniques (e.g., A Posteriori affinity Propagation) to link clusters across timesteps."), mdx("td", null, "If you\u2019re building a tool for historical texts spanning decades, this is your #1 priority.")), mdx("tr", null, mdx("th", null, mdx("b", null, "2. Sense-based interpretability")), mdx("td", null, "\"Sense-based approaches struggle to exactly link clusters to word meanings due to noise/clustering artifacts.\""), mdx("td", null, "Use causal LLMs or sense-prototypes to generate semantic descriptions (not just keywords) for clusters."), mdx("td", null, "Makes LSC tools usable by linguists\u2014not just as black boxes.")), mdx("tr", null, mdx("th", null, mdx("b", null, "3. Multilingual LSC gaps")), mdx("td", null, "\"Most work uses monolingual models; there\u2019s no systematic comparison of multilingual vs. monolingual LLMs.\""), mdx("td", null, "Run cross-lingual experiments (e.g., test GEMS English + Norwegian LSC on shared word clusters)."), mdx("td", null, "Critical for global lexicography or historical language contact studies.")), mdx("tr", null, mdx("th", null, mdx("b", null, "4. Integration with linguistic theory")), mdx("td", null, "\"90%+ of papers don\u2019t link semantic change to real linguistic laws (e.g., amelioration, metaphorization).\""), mdx("td", null, "Map LSC results to established semantic change laws (e.g., \"This shift matches pejoration in English\")."), mdx("td", null, "Turns LSC from a metric into a tool for actual linguistic discovery.")), mdx("tr", null, mdx("th", null, mdx("b", null, "5. Model bias in historical contexts")), mdx("td", null, "\"Orthographic biases (e.g., casing, accents) distort LSC for languages like Italian or German.\""), mdx("td", null, "Implement language-specific bias fixes (e.g., lemmatization only on target words, not entire corpora)."), mdx("td", null, "Fixes false positives in historical analysis (e.g., conflating \"papa\" and \"pap\xE0\").")))), mdx("p", null, "\uD83D\uDCA1 Why These Are the Most Urgent Next Steps (Based on the Paper)\nThe authors don\u2019t just list problems\u2014they rank them by feasibility and impact. Here\u2019s what they\u2019d prioritize first:\nStart with meaning evolution tracking (Problem #1):"), mdx("p", null, " \"The most pressing challenge is extending evaluation frameworks to multiple time periods and building incremental techniques for dynamic corpora.\"\n\u2192 Your first action: If you\u2019re working with time-series data (>2 intervals), prototype an incremental clustering method now."), mdx("p", null, "Fix sense-based interpretability (Problem #2):"), mdx("p", null, " \"Causal LLMs have been proposed to generate descriptive cluster interpretations\u2014but we need to scale this to real-world corpora.\"\n\u2192 Your first action: Add a tiny LLM (e.g., Llama-2) to auto-generate semantic explanations for sense clusters."), mdx("p", null, "Test multilingual LSC (Problem #3):"), mdx("p", null, " \"We argue that a systematic comparison of monolingual vs. multilingual models is required to determine where one outperforms the other.\"\n\u2192 Your first action: Run a pilot experiment comparing BERT-tiny (English) vs. mBERT (multilingual) on a shared corpus like COHA."), mdx("p", null, "\uD83D\uDE80 What I\u2019d Do Right Now as a Practitioner (Using This Paper)\nIf I were building an LSC tool for historical Norwegian lexicography (based on the paper\u2019s NOR Norwegian data), my next steps would be:\nUse the incremental clustering technique from Section 6.1 to track meaning evolution across 100-year intervals.\nApply lemmatization only to target words (not entire texts) to fix orthographic bias (Section 6.3).\nMap clusters to Norwegian semantic change laws (e.g., \"narrowing\" vs. \"broadening\") using the paper\u2019s framework.\nThis isn\u2019t theoretical\u2014it\u2019s exactly what the survey says will move LSC from \"benchmarks\" to \"linguistic tools.\""), mdx("p", null, "\uD83D\uDCDD Final Summary for You (The User)\nThe biggest problem remaining? We still can\u2019t reliably tell which specific meaning changed over time in a language that\u2019s been studied for centuries.\nThe next step for you? Start with one of these:\nImplement incremental clustering for multi-interval tracking (e.g., using APP from References ", "[22,105]", "),\nBuild a sense-prototype generator to turn clusters into linguistic explanations,\nRun a monolingual vs. multilingual LSC test on your target language.\nThe paper doesn\u2019t just say \"this is hard\"\u2014it gives you the exact tools and experiments to start solving it today. If you\u2019re ready, I can draft a step-by-step plan for any of these next steps you choose (e.g., \"How to implement incremental clustering in Python\").\nJust reply with your language/corpus, and I\u2019ll show you exactly where to begin. \uD83D\uDE0A"), mdx("h5", {
