  }))), "Experience using the LLM"), mdx("p", null, "One thing I found interesting is how the LLM seemed to make and then (at least somewhat) correct a similar mistake that I made as a reader. It started by really emphasizing that the paper\u2019s classification framework had three dimensions, meaning representation, time awareness, and learning modality, which I also did in my initial read, simply because they lay this out at the beginning of the paper. Later, however, I updated my description with a focus almost entirely on the form-based vs sense-based distinction as this is the organizing principle behind almost the entire paper after the first section (time awareness and learning modality become simply features among many others in the tables comparing the approaches). Jan-v1-4b eventually seemed to come to the same conclusion. By the time I was asking \u201CWhat are the results and how do they compare with competing approaches?\u201D the other parts of the classification framework faded away, and the LLM focused entirely on the form/sense distinction.\nI gave Jan the custom instructions, \u201CYou are an NLP expert who is going to explain in technical detail the inner workings of academic NLP literature.\u201D Initially, I believed that this custom instruction was leading the LLM to not shy away from the more technical aspects of the paper. Unfortunately, however, after several prompts I came to the conclusion that it didn\u2019t seem to be more technical or detailed in its output than other LLMs.\nI was excited to see that of the many possible takeaways from this paper, the LLM focused on one that is also of interest to me that I wrote about in my description. In the LLM\u2019s words:\n\u201CThe paper notes LSC is \"still a theoretical problem not yet integrated in real application scenarios\" (e.g., historical information retrieval, lexicography). The authors want to identify actionable pathways to move beyond theory toward tools that solve concrete problems (e.g., \"How can LSC help detect semantic shifts in public opinion during historical events?\").\u201D"), mdx("h4", {
